\id ECC - Brenton English Septuagint 
\h Ecclesiastes 
\toc1 Ecclesiastes 
\toc2 Ecclesiastes 
\toc3 Eccl. 
\mt1 ECCLESIASTES 
\c 1 
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* words of the Preacher, the son of David, king of Israel in Jerusalem. 
\v 2 Vanity of vanities, said the Preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity. 
\p
\v 3 What advantage \add is there\add* for a man in all his labour that he undertakes under the sun? 
\v 4 A generation goes, and a generation comes: but the earth stands forever. 
\v 5 The sun rises, and the sun sets and draws toward its place; 
\v 6 rising there, it proceeds southward, and goes round toward the north. The wind goes round and round, and the wind returns to its circuits. 
\v 7 All the rivers run into the sea; and yet the sea isn't filled: to the place where the rivers come, there they return again. 
\v 8 All matters are wearisome; a man will not be able to speak \add of them\add*: neither will the eye be satisfied with seeing, neither will the ear be filled with hearing. 
\p
\v 9 What is it that has been? The very thing that will be: and what is it that has been done? The very thing that will be done: and there is no new thing under the sun. 
\v 10 Who will speak and say, Behold, this is new? It has already been in the ages that have passed before us. 
\v 11 There is no remembrance of the former things; neither will there be remembrance of the latter things among those who will come afterward at the last \add time\add*.\p
\v 12 I, the Preacher, was king over Israel in Jerusalem. 
\v 13 I applied my heart to seek out and examine by wisdom concerning all things that are done under heaven, for God has given to the sons of men an evil trouble to be troubled with. 
\p
\v 14 I beheld all the works that were worked under the sun; and, behold, all were vanity and waywardness of wind. 
\v 15 That which is crooked cannot be made straight: and deficiency cannot be numbered. 
\v 16 I spoke in my heart, saying, Behold, I have increased, and have acquired wisdom beyond all who were before me in Jerusalem, and my heart saw much wisdom and knowledge. 
\v 17 I applied my heart to know wisdom and knowledge, parables and understanding: I perceived that this also is waywardness of wind. 
\v 18 For in the abundance of wisdom is abundance of knowledge; and he who increases knowledge will increase sorrow. 
\c 2 
\p
\v 1 I said in my heart, Come now, I will test you with mirth, and look upon what is good: and, behold, this also is vanity.
\v 2 I said to laughter, Madness: and to mirth, Why do you do this? 
\p
\v 3 I examined in my heart to draw my flesh to wine (though my heart guided \add me\add* in wisdom), and \add I desired\add* to lay hold of mirth, until I should see what kind of good the sons of men will do under the sun all the days of their life.
\v 4 I enlarged my work; I built houses for myself; I planted vineyards for myself. 
\v 5 I made myself gardens and orchards, and planted in them every kind of fruit-tree. 
\v 6 I made myself pools of water, to water the grove that sprouts trees from them. 
\v 7 I got servants and maidens, and servants were born to me in the house: also I had abundant possession of flocks and herds, beyond all who were before me in Jerusalem. 
\v 8 Moreover I collected for myself silver and gold, and the treasures of kings and provinces: I procured for myself singing men and singing women, and delights of the sons of men, a butler and female cupbearers. 
\p
\v 9 So I became great, and advanced beyond all that were before me in Jerusalem: also my wisdom continued with me. 
\v 10 Whatever my eyes desired, I didn't withhold from them; I didn't withhold my heart from all mirth: for my heart rejoiced in all my labour; and this was my share of all my labour. 
\v 11 I looked on all my works which my hands worked, and on my labour which I laboured to perform: and, behold, all was vanity and waywardness of wind, and there is no advantage under the sun. 
\p
\v 12 Then I looked to see wisdom, and madness, and folly: for who is the man who will follow after counsel, in all the things in which he employed it?
\v 13 I saw that wisdom excels folly, as much as light excels darkness. 
\v 14 The wise man's eyes are in his head; but the fool walks in darkness: and I, even I, perceived that one event will happen to them all. 
\p
\v 15 I said in my heart, As the event of the fool is, so will it happen to me, even to me; and why have I gained wisdom? I said moreover in my heart, This is also vanity, because the fool speaks from abundance. 
\v 16 For there is no remembrance of the wise man with the fool forever; because now \add in\add* the coming days all things are forgotten: and how will the wise man die with the fool? 
\p
\v 17 So I hated life; because the work that was worked under the sun was evil before me: for all is vanity and waywardness of wind. 
\v 18 I hated the whole of my labour which I labour under the sun; because I must leave it to the man who will come after me. 
\v 19 Who knows whether he will be wise or a fool? And whether he will have power over all my labour in which I laboured, and in which I grew wise under the sun? This is also vanity. 
\v 20 So I turned to dismiss from my heart all my labour in which I had laboured under the sun. 
\v 21 For there is a man whose labour is in wisdom, and in knowledge, and in skill; yet he will give his portion to one who has not laboured in it. This is also vanity and great evil. 
\v 22 For what comes to a man from all his labour, and from the purpose of his heart in which he labours under the sun? 
\v 23 For all his days \add are days\add* of sorrows, and his trouble is vexation; in the night also his heart doesn't rest. This is also vanity. 
\p
\v 24 There is nothing good for a man except to eat, and to drink, and to show his soul \add as\add* good in his trouble. This also I saw, that it is from the hand of God. 
\v 25 For who will eat, or who will drink, without him? 
\v 26 For \add God\add* has given to the man who is good in his sight wisdom, and knowledge, and joy: but he has given to the sinner trouble, to add and to heap up, that he may give to the man who is good before God; for this is also vanity and waywardness of wind. 
\c 3 
\p
\v 1 To all things there is a time, and a season for every matter under heaven.
\q1
\v 2 A time to give birth,
\q2 and a time to die; 
\q1 a time to plant, 
\q2 and a time to pluck up what has been planted; 
\q1
\v 3 a time to kill, 
\q2 and a time to heal; 
\q1 a time to pull down, 
\q2 and a time to build up; 
\q1
\v 4 a time to weep, 
\q2 and a time to laugh; 
\q1 a time to lament, 
\q2 and a time to dance; 
\q1
\v 5 a time to throw stones, 
\q2 and a time to gather stones together;
\q1 a time to embrace,
\q2 and a time to abstain from embracing; 
\q1
\v 6 a time to seek,
\q2 and a time to lose;
\q1 a time to keep,
\q2 and a time to cast away; 
\q1
\v 7 a time to tear,
\q2 and a time to sew;
\q1 a time to be silent,
\q2 and a time to speak;
\q1 
\v 8 a time to love,
\q2 and a time to hate;
\q1 a time of war,
\q2 and a time of peace. 
\p
\v 9 What advantage \add is there\add* for him who works in those things in which he labours? 
\p
\v 10 I have seen all the trouble that God has given to the sons of men to be troubled with. 
\v 11 All the things which he has made are beautiful in his time: he has also set eternity in their heart, that man might not find out the work which God has worked from the beginning even to the end. 
\v 12 I know that there is no good in them, except \add for a man\add* to rejoice, and to do good in his life. 
\p
\v 13 Also every man who eats and drinks, and sees good in all his labour, \add this\add* is a gift of God. 
\v 14 I know that whatever things God has done, they will be forever: it is impossible to add to it, and it is impossible to take away from it: and God has done \add it\add*, that \add men\add* may fear before him. 
\v 15 That which has been is now; and whatever things are to be have already been; and God will seek out that which is past. 
\p
\v 16 Moreover I saw under the sun the place of judgment, there was the ungodly one; and the place of righteousness, there was the ungodly one. 
\v 17 I said in my heart, God will judge the righteous and the ungodly: for there is a time there for every action and for every work. 
\p
\v 18 I said in my heart, concerning the speech of the sons of men, God will judge them to show that they are beasts. 
\v 19 Also what befalls the sons of man befalls the brute; one thing befalls them: as is the death of the one, so also the death of the other; and there is one breath to all: and what has man more than the brute? Nothing; for all is vanity.
\v 20 All \add go\add* to one place; all came from the dust, and all will return to dust. 
\v 21 Who knows the \f + \fr 3:21 \fqa Or, \ft breath.\f*spirit of the sons of man, whether it goes upward? And the spirit of the beast, whether it goes downward to the earth? 
\v 22 I saw that there was no good except that a man should rejoice in his works, for it is his share, for who will bring him to see anything of that which will be after him? 
\c 4 
\p
\v 1 So I returned, and saw all the oppressions that were done under the sun: and behold the tear of the oppressed, and they had no comforter; and on the side of those who oppressed them was power; but they had no comforter: 
\v 2 and I praised all the dead that had already died more than the living, as many as are alive until now. 
\v 3 Better also than both is he who has not yet been, who has not seen the evil work that is done under the sun. 
\p
\v 4 I saw all labour, and all the diligent work, that it is a man's envy of his neighbour. This is also vanity and waywardness of wind. 
\v 5 The fool folds his hands together, and eats his own flesh. 
\v 6 Better is a handful of rest than two handfuls of labour and waywardness of wind. 
\p
\v 7 So I returned, and saw vanity under the sun. 
\v 8 There is one \add alone\add*, and there isn't a second; yes, he has neither son nor brother: yet there is no end to all his labour; neither is his eye satisfied with wealth; and for whom do I labour, and deprive my soul of good? This is also vanity, and an evil trouble. 
\v 9 Two \add are\add* better than one: they have a good reward for their labour. 
\v 10 For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falls, and there isn't a second to lift him up. 
\v 11 Also if two should lie together, they also get heat: but how will one be warmed \add alone\add*? 
\v 12 If one should prevail against \add him\add*, the two will withstand him; and a three-stranded cord will not be quickly broken. 
\p
\v 13 Better is a poor and wise child than an old and foolish king, who doesn't know how to take heed any longer. 
\v 14 For he will come out of the house of prisoners to reign, though he was born poor in his kingdom.
\v 15 I beheld all the living who were walking under the sun, with the second youth who will stand up in his place. 
\v 16 There is no end to all the people, to all who were before them: yet those who come afterward will not rejoice in him: for this also is vanity and waywardness of wind.
\p
\v 17 Keep your foot, whenever you go to the house of God and are near to hear; your sacrifice is better than the gift of fools: for they don't know that they are doing evil. 
\c 5 
\p
\v 1 Don't be hasty with your mouth, and don't let your heart be swift to utter a word before God; for God is in heaven, and you upon earth: therefore let your words be few. 
\v 2 For through the multitude of trouble a dream comes; and a fool's voice is with a multitude of words. 
\p
\v 3 Whenever you vow a vow to God, don't defer paying it; for \add he has\add* no pleasure in fools: therefore pay whatever you have vowed. 
\v 4 Better that you should not vow, than that you should vow and not pay. 
\v 5 Don't permit your mouth to lead your flesh to sin; and don't say in the presence of God, It was a mistake: lest God be angry at your voice, and destroy the works of your hands. 
\v 6 For in a multitude of dreams there are also vanities and many words: but fear God.
\p
\v 7 If you should see the oppression of the poor, and the wresting of judgment and of justice in the land, don't wonder at the matter: for a high one watches over him who is high, and high ones over them.
\v 8 Also the abundance of the earth is for every one: the king \add is dependent on\add* the cultivated field. 
\p
\v 9 He who loves silver will not be satisfied with silver. Who has loved gain in its abundance? This is also vanity. 
\v 10 In the multitude of good, those who eat it are increased: and what advantage has the owner, except the right of beholding \add it\add* with his eyes? 
\v 11 The sleep of a servant is sweet, whether he eats little or much: but to one who is satiated with wealth, no one lets him sleep.
\p
\v 12 There is an affliction which I have seen under the sun, \add namely\add*, wealth kept for its owner to his hurt. 
\v 13 That wealth will perish in an evil trouble: and \add the man\add* begets a son, and there is nothing in his hand. 
\v 14 As he came forth naked from his mother's womb, he will return as he came, and he will receive nothing for his labour, that it should go \add with him\add* in his hand. 
\v 15 This is also an evil affliction: for as he came, so also will he return: and what is his gain, for which he vainly labours? 
\v 16 Yes, all his days are in darkness, and in mourning, and much sorrow, and affliction, and wrath. 
\p
\v 17 Behold, what I have seen is good, and it is a fine thing \add for a man\add* to eat and to drink, and to see good in all his labour in which he may labour under the sun, the number of the days of his life which God has given to him: for it is his share.
\v 18 Yes, every man to whom God has given wealth and possessions, and has given him power to eat of it, and to receive his share, and to rejoice in his labour; this is the gift of God. 
\v 19 For he will not much remember the days of his life; for God occupies him with the mirth of his heart. 
\c 6 
\p
\v 1 There is an evil which I have seen under the sun, and it lies heavily upon man: 
\v 2 a man to whom God will give wealth, and substance, and honour, and he lacks nothing for his soul of all things that he desires, yet God will not give him power to eat of it, for a stranger will devour it: this is vanity, and an evil affliction. 
\p
\v 3 A man might father a hundred \add children\add*, and live many years, yes, however abundant the days of his years might be, yet \add if\add* his soul is not satisfied with good, and he has no burial, I said, An untimely birth is better than him. 
\v 4 For he came in vanity, and departs in darkness, and his name will be covered in darkness. 
\v 5 Moreover he has not seen the sun, nor known \add anything\add*: there is more rest for this one than for that one.
\v 6 Though he has lived twice a thousand years, yet he has seen no good: do not all go to one place? 
\p
\v 7 All the labour of a man is for his mouth, and yet the appetite will not be satisfied. 
\v 8 For what advantage has the wise man over the fool? For the poor knows how to walk in the face of life.
\v 9 The sight of the eyes is better than the wandering of the soul: this is also vanity, and waywardness of wind.
\p
\v 10 If anything has been, its name has already been called: and it is known what man is; neither can he contend with him who is stronger than he. 
\v 11 For there are many things that increase vanity. 
\p
\v 12 For who knows \add what is\add* good for a man in life, \add during\add* the number of the days of his life of vanity? And he has spent them as a shadow; for who will tell a man what will be after him under the sun? 
\c 7 
\p
\q1
\v 1 A good name is better than good oil;
\q2 and the day of death than the day of birth.
\q1 
\v 2 Better to go to the house of mourning, 
\q2 than to go to the banquet house: 
\q2 since this is the end of every man; 
\q2 and the living man will lay it to his heart. 
\q1
\v 3 Sorrow is better than laughter: 
\q2 for by the sadness of the face the heart will be made better. 
\q1
\v 4 The heart of the wise is in the house of mourning; 
\q2 but the heart of fools is in the house of mirth. 
\p
\q1
\v 5 Better to hear a reproof from a wise man,
\q2 than for a man to hear the song of fools.
\q1 
\v 6 As the sound of thorns under a caldron,
\q2 so is the laughter of fools:
\q3 this is also vanity. 
\q1
\v 7 For oppression makes a wise man mad,
\q2 and destroys his steadfast heart.
\q1 
\v 8 The end of a matter is better than the beginning of it:
\q2 the patient is better than the high-minded.
\q1 
\v 9 Don't be hasty in your spirit to be angry:
\q2 for anger will rest in the bosom of fools.
\q1 
\v 10 Don't say, What has happened, that the former days were better than these?
\q2 For you do not enquire in wisdom concerning this. 
\p
\q1
\v 11 Wisdom is good with an inheritance:
\q2 and an advantage to those who see the sun.
\q1
\v 12 For wisdom in its shadow is as the shadow of silver:
\q2 and the advantage of the knowledge of wisdom will give life to him who has it. 
\p
\q1
\v 13 Behold the works of God:
\q2 for who can straighten him whom God has made crooked? 
\q1
\v 14 In the day of prosperity live joyfully,
\q2 and consider in the day of adversity:
\q1 God also has caused the one to agree with the other,
\q2 that man should find nothing after him.
\p
\q1
\v 15 I have seen all things in the days of my vanity:
\q2 there is a just man perishing in his justice,
\q2 and there is an ungodly man remaining in his wickedness. 
\q1
\v 16 Don't be very just; neither be very wise:
\q2 lest you be greatly agitated. 
\q1
\v 17 Don't be very wicked; and don't be hard:
\q2 lest you should die before your time. 
\q1
\v 18 It is well for you to hold fast by this;
\q2 also by this don't withdraw your hand:
\q2 for he who fears God will come forth through them all.
\p
\q1
\v 19 Wisdom will help the wise man more than ten mighty men who are in the city. 
\q1
\v 20 For there isn't a righteous man in the earth, who will do good, and not sin.
\q1 
\v 21 Also take no heed to all the words that are spoken;
\q2 lest you hear your servant cursing you. 
\q1
\v 22 For many times he will trespass against you,
\q2 and repeatedly he will afflict your heart;
\q2 for thus also have you cursed others. 
\q1
\v 23 All these things have I proved in wisdom:
\q2 I said, I will be wise; but it was far from me. 
\q1
\v 24 \add That which is\add* far beyond what was,
\q2 and a great depth, who will find it out? 
\p
\v 25 I went about in my heart to know, and to examine, and to seek wisdom, and the account \add of things\add*, and to know the folly and hardness and madness of the ungodly.
\p
\v 26 And I find more bitter than death the woman who is a snare, whose heart is traps and nets, and bands in her hands: he who is good in the sight of God will be delivered from her; but the sinner will be caught by her.
\v 27 Behold, this have I found, said the Preacher, one by one, to find out the account,
\v 28 which my soul sought after, but I didn't find: for I have found one man of a thousand; but a woman in all these I have not found. 
\v 29 But, behold, this have I found, that God made man upright; but they have sought out many schemes. 
\c 8 
\p
\v 1 Who knows the wise? And who knows the interpretation of a saying? 
\p A man's wisdom will lighten his face; but a man of shameless face will be hated. 
\p
\v 2 Keep the command of the king, and don't be hasty concerning the matter of the oath of God.
\v 3 Go forth from his presence: don't stand in an evil matter; for he will do whatever he pleases, 
\v 4 for the king speaks with authority: and who will say to him, What are you doing?
\p
\v 5 He who keeps the commandment will not know an evil thing: and the heart of the wise knows the time of judgment. 
\v 6 For to every thing there is time and judgment; for the knowledge of a man lies heavily upon him. 
\v 7 For no one knows what is going to be: for who will tell him how it will be?
\p
\v 8 There is no man who has power over the spirit to retain the spirit; and there is no power in the day of death: and there is no discharge in the day of battle; neither will ungodliness save the one who belongs to it.
\p
\v 9 So I saw all this, and I applied my heart to every work that has been done under the sun; all the things in which man has power over man to afflict him. 
\v 10 Then I saw the ungodly carried into the tombs, and they went from the holy place, and were praised in the city, because they had done thus: this also is vanity.
\p
\v 11 Because no response is quickly made against those who do evil, therefore the heart of the children of men is fully determined in them to do evil.
\v 12 Though the sinner has done evil from then onward and for a long time, nevertheless I know that it is well with those who fear God, so they may fear before him:
\v 13 but it will not be well with the ungodly, and he will not prolong his days, \add which are\add* as a shadow; because he doesn't fear before God. 
\p
\v 14 There is a vanity which is done upon the earth: there are righteous persons to whom it happens according to the acts of the ungodly; and there are ungodly men to whom it happens according to the acts of the righteous. I said, This is also vanity.
\v 15 Then I praised mirth, because there is no good for a man under the sun, except to eat, and to drink, and to be merry: and this will accompany him in his labour all the days of his life, which God has given him under the sun.
\p
\v 16 When I applied my heart to know wisdom, and to perceive the trouble that is worked upon the earth—for even by day and by night a man sees no sleep with his eyes—
\v 17 I beheld all the works of God, that a man will not be able to discover the work which is worked under the sun. However much a man labours to seek it, he will not find it; yes, even if a wise man should say that he knows it, he will not be able to find it.
\c 9 
\p
\v 1 For I laid all this to my heart, and my heart saw all this: that the righteous, and the wise, and their works, are in the hand of God; yet man knows neither love nor hatred: all things are before them.
\v 2 Vanity is in all: one fate comes to the righteous, and to the wicked; to the good, and to the bad; both to the pure, and to the impure; both to him who sacrifices, and to him who doesn't sacrifice: as is the good, so is the sinner: as is the one who swears an oath, even so is he who fears an oath.
\p
\v 3 This is an evil in all that is done under the sun, that one fate comes to all: yes, the heart of the sons of men is filled with evil, and madness is in their heart during their life, and after that \add they go\add* to the dead. 
\v 4 For whoever is joined with all the living has hope: for a living dog is better than a dead lion. 
\v 5 For the living will know that they will die: but the dead know nothing, and there is no longer any reward for them; for their memory is lost. 
\v 6 Also their love, and their hatred, and their envy, have now perished; yes, there is no share for them any more, forever, in all that is done under the sun. 
\p
\v 7 Go, eat your bread with mirth, and drink your wine with a joyful heart; for now God has favourably accepted your works. 
\v 8 Let your garments be always white; and don't let oil be lacking on your head. 
\v 9 See life with the wife whom you love all the days of the life of your vanity, which are given you under the sun: for that is your share in your life, and in your labour in which you labour under the sun.
\p
\v 10 Whatever your hand will find to do, do with all your might; for there is no work, nor scheme, nor knowledge, nor wisdom, in Hades where you are going. 
\p
\v 11 I returned, and saw under the sun, that the race isn't to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, nor yet bread to the wise, nor yet wealth to men of understanding, nor yet favour to men of knowledge; for time and events come to them all. 
\v 12 For surely man also doesn't know his time: as fish that are taken in an evil net, and as birds that are caught in a snare; even so the sons of men are snared at an evil time, when it falls suddenly upon them. 
\p
\v 13 This I also saw \add to be\add* wisdom under the sun, and it is great before me: 
\v 14 \add there was\add* a little city, and few men in it; and there came against it a great king, and surrounded it, and built great mounds against it;
\v 15 and there was found in it a poor wise man, and he saved the city through his wisdom: yet no one remembered that poor man. 
\v 16 I said, Wisdom is better than power: yet the wisdom of the poor man is despised, and his words are not listened to. 
\p
\v 17 The words of the wise are heard in quiet more than the cry of those who rule in folly. 
\p
\v 18 Wisdom is better than weapons of war: and one sinner will destroy much good. 
\c 10 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Deadly flies corrupt a preparation of sweet ointment:
\q2 a little wisdom is more precious than the great glory of folly. 
\p
\q1
\v 2 A wise man's heart is at his right hand; 
\q2 but a fool's heart at his left. 
\q1
\v 3 Yes, and whenever a fool walks by the way, his heart is lacking,
\q2 and all that he thinks of is folly. 
\p
\q1
\v 4 If the spirit of the ruler rise up against you, don't leave your place;
\q2 for soothing will put an end to great offences. 
\q1
\v 5 There is an evil which I have seen under the sun,
\q2 wherein an error has proceeded from the ruler. 
\q1
\v 6 The fool has been set in very high places,
\q2 and rich men sit in a low place. 
\q1
\v 7 I have seen servants upon horses,
\q2 and princes walking as servants on the earth. 
\p
\q1
\v 8 He who digs a pit will fall into it;
\q2 and he who breaks down a hedge, a snake will bite. 
\p
\q1
\v 9 He who removes stones will be troubled by them;
\q2 he who splits wood will be endangered by it. 
\p
\q1
\v 10 If the iron falls away,
\q2 and he has disturbed the face,
\q1 then he must strengthen his strength:
\q2 but wisdom is the advantage of skill. 
\p
\q1
\v 11 If a snake bite when there is no \add charmer's\add* whisper,
\q2 then there is no advantage to the charmer. 
\q1
\v 12 The words of a wise mouth are gracious:
\q2 but the lips of a fool will swallow him up. 
\q1
\v 13 The beginning of the words of his mouth is folly:
\q2 and the end of his talk evil madness. 
\q1
\v 14 A fool moreover multiplies words:
\q2 man doesn't know what has been, nor what will be:
\q2 who will tell him what will come after him? 
\q1
\v 15 The labour of fools will afflict them,
\q2 because they do not know how to go to the city. 
\p
\q1
\v 16 Woe to you, O city, whose king is young,
\q2 and your princes eat in the morning! 
\q1
\v 17 You are blessed, O land, whose king is a son of nobles,
\q2 and whose princes will eat seasonably, for strength,
\q2 and will not be ashamed. 
\p
\q1
\v 18 By slothful neglect the roof-beam will be brought low:
\q2 and by idleness of the hands the house will fall to pieces. 
\p
\q1
\v 19 Men prepare bread for laughter,
\q2 and wine gladdens the living:
\q2 but all things answer to money. 
\p
\q1
\v 20 Even in your conscience, don't curse the king;
\q2 and don't curse the rich in your bedchamber:
\q1 for a bird of the air will carry your voice,
\q2 and that which has wings will report your speech. 
\c 11 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Send forth your bread upon the face of the water:
\q2 for you will find it after many days. 
\q1
\v 2 Give a share to seven, and also to eight;
\q2 for you know not what evil there will be upon the earth. 
\q1
\v 3 If the clouds are filled with rain,
\q2 they will pour out upon the earth:
\q1 and if a tree falls southward, or if it falls northward,
\q2 in the place where the tree falls, there it will be.
\q1 
\v 4 He who observes the wind doesn't sow;
\q2 and he who looks at the clouds will not reap. 
\q1
\v 5 In these things there is no one who knows the way of the wind:
\q2 as the bones in the womb of her who is pregnant,
\q2 so you will not know the works of God, who does all things.
\q1 
\v 6 In the morning sow your seed, and in the evening don't let your hand be slack:
\q2 for you don't know what sort will prosper, whether this or that, or whether both will be good alike. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 Moreover the light is sweet,
\q2 and it is good for the eyes to see the sun. 
\q1
\v 8 For even if a man should live many years,
\q2 \add and\add* rejoice in them all;
\q1 yet let him remember the days of darkness;
\q2 for they will be many.
\q3 All that comes is vanity. 
\p
\v 9 Rejoice, O young man, in your youth; and let your heart cheer you in the days of your youth, and walk in the ways of your heart and in the sight of your eyes: yet know that for all these things God will bring you into judgment. 
\v 10 Therefore remove anger from your heart, and put away evil from your flesh: for youth and folly are vanity. 
\c 12 
\p
\v 1 Remember your Creator in the days of your youth, before the days of evil come, and the years overtake \add you\add* in which you will say, I have no pleasure in them,
\v 2 before the sun and the light and the moon and the stars are darkened, and the clouds return after the rain: 
\v 3 in the day in which the keepers of the house tremble, and the mighty men become bent, and the grinding \add women\add* cease because they have become few, and those looking through the openings grow dark; 
\v 4 and they shut the doors in the market-place, because of the weakness of the voice of her that grinds \add at the mill\add*; and he rises up at the voice of the sparrow, and all the daughters of song are brought low; 
\v 5 and they look from on high, and fears \add are\add* in the way, and the almond tree blossoms, and the locust grows fat, and the caper bursts: because man has gone to his eternal home, and the mourners have gone about the market: 
\v 6 before the silver cord is loosed, or the golden ornament is broken, or the pitcher is broken at the fountain, or the wheel is shattered at the cistern; 
\v 7 \add before\add* the dust also returns to the earth as it was, and the spirit returns to God who gave it. 
\p
\v 8 Vanity of vanities, said the Preacher; all is vanity. 
\v 9 And beyond this, because the Preacher was wise, he still taught knowledge to the people, and the ear will trace out an ordered arrangement of parables.
\v 10 The Preacher sought diligently to find out acceptable words, and an upright writing, \add even\add* words of truth. 
\v 11 The words of the wise are as goads, and as nails firmly fastened, which were gathered together from one shepherd.
\v 12 Moreover, my son, guard yourself by means of them: of making many books there is no end; and much study is a weariness of the flesh. 
\p
\v 13 Hear the end of the matter, the sum: Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is for every man. 
\v 14 For God will bring every work into judgment, with everything that has been overlooked, whether \add it be\add* good, or whether \add it be\add* evil. 
